<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cancuén</h1><p class="st-meta">650 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Cancuén</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya trading kingdom at the head of navigation on the Pasión river, rich from jade and obsidian, which ended in a massacre of its royal court around 800.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cancuén lay where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasion-river">Pasión</a> river becomes navigable, at the gateway between the Guatemalan highlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> lowlands. It rose to prominence in the 7th century, around 650, under the patronage of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a>, whose overlord oversaw the accession of a local king in 656.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Instead of a great temple pyramid, Cancuén's core was a vast palace of some 170 rooms. The city worked jade, pyrite and obsidian brought from the highlands, and controlled river trade. Its greatest king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-chan-ahk">Taj Chan Ahk</a>, who reigned from 757 to about 795, built much of the palace and a ballcourt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 800 the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kan-maax">Kan Maax</a>, his queen and some 30 nobles were killed and their bodies placed in a cistern, and the city was abandoned. The massacre, found in 2005, is vivid evidence of the violence of the Classic collapse.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cancuen.jpg" alt="Cancuén" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Authenticmaya · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cancuenpanel3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cancuén</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Taj Chan Ahk, Kan Maax</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cancuen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=725&amp;focus=cancuen" aria-label="Cancuén on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2225 1582 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cancuén lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2545 1838L2564 1762"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2564 1762h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2545 1838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2545 1838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=725&amp;focus=cancuen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

